    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The diagonal of the square is 5 inches, so each side of the square is $s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.  The area of the square is $s^2 = \\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{75}{4} = 18.75$ square inches.\n\nThe diameter of the circle is 5 inches, so the radius is $r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5$ inches.  The area of the circle is $\\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = \\frac{25\\pi}{4}$.  To find the difference in area between the circle and the square, we subtract the square's area from the circle's area:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{25\\pi}{4} - 18.75} \\approx 6.2 - 18.75 = \\boxed{-12.55}\n\\]\nThe answer is: -12.55",
